# Differential cases (ADR-0020) of #112 "S2b.9 Cardinality, ending at its result" (ADR-0007 and its #112 amendment,
# F-56): "one" and "at most one" also promise that nothing runs after the result, so a node that may run on after it
# is "many". These are F-56's three jq-ref probes; a backend that took any of them for a value form or a tail jump would
# drop the rest. Every case is required at #112 (oracle/src/test/resources/require/112.txt). Format:
# oracle/src/main/java/io/github/subapavel/jqj/oracle/diff/DiffCase.java. Expected outputs live in cardinality.rec,
# written by scripts/oracle-record.sh only. Ids are immutable. Every case uses -c.

# limit stops after the first result: running the rest of (1, error("x")) first would raise x instead of [2].

case cardinality/limit-stops-before-the-error
arg -c
filter [limit(1; (1, error("x")) | .+1)]
stdin null\n
end

# first stops after the first result: running the rest first would never return (repeat(1)|empty is endless).

case cardinality/first-stops-before-the-endless-rest
arg -c
filter first((1, (repeat(1)|empty)) as $x | $x)
stdin null\n
end

# A recursive call after a comma: each pending error x is raised after the result 4 (rc 5); a tail jump would drop it.

case cardinality/recursion-after-a-comma-keeps-the-pending-errors
arg -c
filter def f: if .>3 then . else ((.+1), error("x")) | f end; 0|f
stdin null\n
end
